# The fontTools oracle: the reference for "does this table parse to the same
# fields", and the image that also builds this library's fixtures.
#
# documentation/design.md section 14.7. Everything the build reads is pinned,
# because this image is **built here** and two builds of one Containerfile are
# not two copies of one image. So the
# base is pinned by digest, the Python packages by exact version, and Debian's
# font packages by full apt version - and the run-time version check in
# oracle_env.py is the real guarantee, which is why it runs in both modes here
# where `unicode` was able to relax it.
#
# The apt pins will eventually fail to resolve when the archive moves past
# them. That is the intended failure: a bare `apt-get install fonts-dejavu-core`
# would silently install a different DejaVu and report the corpus as changed.
# snapshot.debian.org is the durable answer when it first happens; it is not
# worth the weight before then.
FROM docker.io/library/python:3.14-slim@sha256:7bf6c3111fe094f8ee1a1cbcdc63c4cfb345b0e3df42d5aa9a90b3b4b022ab6d

# The locale, written down rather than inherited. design.md section 14.7 says
# every built-here image pins C.UTF-8, and this one did not: the base image
# leaves LANG unset and the interpreter happened to choose UTF-8 on its own,
# which is a default and not a pin. An unset LANG produced fifty false
# disagreements in the vim oracle. Two paths here are exposed to it: the Mac
# Roman name records the fixture generator encodes, and any byte a differential
# prints. Neither should depend on what a future base image decides.
ENV LANG=C.UTF-8 \
    LC_ALL=C.UTF-8 \
    PYTHONHASHSEED=0

# fontTools answers for every table, and brotli is what it needs to read or
# write WOFF 2 - which this library defers (section 16), but the oracle should
# be able to read one when the question comes up.
#
# Pillow is here for a format fontTools does not read at all: it carries PCF and
# BDF readers of its own, descended from PIL's X font support, and they are the
# only second reading of a bitmap container available without building `bdftopcf`
# and `psftools` into an image of their own. `bitmap_diff.py` says exactly which
# half of each format it can serve as a reference for - its BDF reader is sound,
# and its PCF *encoding* is not.
RUN pip install --no-cache-dir --no-compile \
      fonttools==4.66.0 \
      brotli==1.2.0 \
      pillow==12.3.0

# The fonts a differential names. They live **only here**: no third-party font
# is committed to this repository (section 14.6), and the two jobs a corpus does
# want different fonts - the unit fixtures exercise code paths and are this
# library's own, while conformance wants the population that is not.
#
# What each is for:
#   fonts-dejavu-core        TrueType outlines, a `kern` table, five cmap subtables
#   fonts-liberation2        TrueType, a second foundry's idea of the same tables
#   fonts-urw-base35         CFF outlines in an sfnt - the `OTTO` flavour
#   fonts-noto-core          breadth of scripts, and OS/2 version 4 in bulk
#   fonts-noto-color-emoji   cmap format 12 past the BMP, format 14 variation
#                            sequences, and CBDT/CBLC strikes for phase 6
#   xfonts-terminus          PCF bitmap strikes, for phase 1b; nothing reads
#                            them yet and they cost two megabytes
#
# The embedded-bitmap half of the list is below, and it is the whole Debian
# population rather than a sample of it: **589 font packages scanned, 5,927
# distinct sfnt files, 33 carrying EBLC/EBDT and 11 of those also EBSC**
# (notes/font/EBLC.md records the sweep and the command that re-runs it).
#
# An earlier survey probed a handful of *candidate* packages chosen from the guess
# that embedded strikes are a CJK thing, reported "two fonts in all of Debian",
# and was wrong three ways at once: it globbed `*.tt[fc]`, so every `.otb` was
# invisible; it swallowed a font fontTools cannot open in a bare
# `except: continue`; and it only ever walked /usr/share/fonts inside an image
# whose packages that same guess had chosen. Anonymous Pro is a programming font
# and Computer Modern is a text face, and between them they carry the index
# format and the two image formats that survey reported as appearing in no Debian
# font at all.
#
#   fonts-arphic-uming       four faces over one EBLC/EBDT byte range, six
#                            strikes of 27,123 glyphs; index formats 1 and 2
#                            with image formats 5 and 7
#   fonts-mona               the only image format 6 in Debian - and a font whose
#                            EBLC fontTools cannot parse at all (fonttools#317),
#                            so it is the case where the two readers disagree
#                            about whether the font is readable. FreeType reads
#                            it; that is what the freetype image is for
#   fonts-anonymous-pro      **EBSC**, four faces of it: three scale tables each,
#                            7, 8 and 9 ppem all deferring to the 10 ppem strike.
#                            Also index format **3**
#   fonts-wine               **EBSC** from an unrelated producer - Wine's
#                            reimplementations of the Windows core bitmap faces,
#                            seven files carrying one. Two independent producers
#                            of a table is the difference between reading a
#                            format and reading one vendor's habit
#   fonts-terminus-otb       **bitmap-only sfnts**: no `glyf` at all. That is the
#                            one shape in which FreeType reports a strike's own
#                            `sbitLineMetrics` instead of a scaled outline's, so
#                            these four files are the only place that oracle can
#                            speak to a strike's baseline
#   fonts-creep2             a second bitmap-only sfnt, from another producer
#   fonts-cmu                Computer Modern: 115 and 367 index subtables of
#                            image format **2**
#   fonts-terminus           the same design as xfonts-terminus' PCF and as the
#                            .otb above, in a scalable sfnt - one design in three
#                            containers
#   fonts-farsiweb           strikes at 62-66 and 94-96 ppem, and a ppem set with
#                            gaps: the only font here that is not a run of small
#                            consecutive sizes
#   fonts-unikurdweb         strikes stored in **descending** ppem order, 16 then
#                            15 then 13 - what a strike policy that assumed
#                            sorted order gets wrong
#   fonts-wqy-zenhei         EBLC on **face 2 only** of a three-face collection,
#                            44,960 glyphs. The earlier survey named this package
#                            as checked and not carrying the table, and that false
#                            negative is what sent the whole population wrong
#   fonts-konatu             fourteen strikes in one face, 8 to 21 ppem
#   fonts-misaki             a single 8 ppem strike, the smallest here
#   fonts-ukij-uyghur        two more faces of index format 1 with image format 2
# The variable fonts, which are the other half of what "the real fonts" means.
# Before these the image held **no** font with an `fvar` table, so nothing in any
# corpus could say whether a variation was applied correctly: the one thing a
# `gvar` reader is *for* had no population at all. A survey of the Debian font
# packages that ship variable TrueType found these, and they were chosen for
# being different producers rather than for being many:
#
#   fonts-inter-variable     **two axes** - `opsz` and `wght` - so a location is a
#                            point in a plane and not a position on a line, which
#                            is the case where a tuple's scalar is a *product*;
#                            `avar`, `HVAR` and `MVAR`; 2,900 glyphs each, in an
#                            upright and an italic
#   fonts-cascadia-code      twelve files of one design, with `cvar` as well as
#                            `gvar` - Microsoft's producer, and the only one here
#                            whose composites are numerous enough to carry
#                            component-offset deltas in bulk
#   fonts-vazirmatn-variable an Arabic-script face: a third producer, 1,333
#                            glyphs, one axis with `avar`
#   fonts-sahel-variable     one axis and **no `avar`**, so the default
#                            normalisation (linear either side of the default) is
#                            the whole story for it
#
#   fonts-cantarell          the one **CFF2** variable font: `fvar` and `CFF2` and no
#                            `gvar`, so its outlines move through blend operators in
#                            their charstrings rather than through tuples. Not in the
#                            `variable` list, which asks for `gvar`; the `cff2` list
#                            asks for `CFF2`
RUN apt-get update -qq \
    && apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends \
      fonts-dejavu-core=2.37-8 \
      fonts-liberation2=1:2.1.5-3 \
      fonts-urw-base35=20200910-8 \
      fonts-noto-core=20201225-2 \
      fonts-noto-color-emoji=2.051-0+deb13u1 \
      xfonts-terminus=4.48-3.1 \
      fonts-arphic-uming=0.2.20080216.2-11 \
      fonts-mona=1:2.90-1.1 \
      fonts-anonymous-pro=1.003-6 \
      fonts-cmu=0.7.0-5 \
      fonts-creep2=0.0~git20210325.69dc0de+ds-5 \
      fonts-farsiweb=0.4.dfsg-13 \
      fonts-konatu=20121218-11 \
      fonts-misaki=20210505-1 \
      fonts-terminus=1.2.0+ds2-4 \
      fonts-terminus-otb=4.48-3.1 \
      fonts-ukij-uyghur=20110217-4 \
      fonts-unikurdweb=1.0-6 \
      fonts-wine=10.0~repack-6 \
      fonts-wqy-zenhei=0.9.45-8 \
      fonts-inter-variable=4.1+ds-1 \
      fonts-cascadia-code=2407.24-1 \
      fonts-vazirmatn-variable=33.003-3 \
      fonts-sahel-variable=3.4.0-3 \
      fonts-cantarell=0.303.1-4 \
    && rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*

# Wine puts its fonts in /usr/share/wine/fonts, which is not /usr/share/fonts, so
# every corpus list program in this image walked past seven of the eleven EBSC
# faces in Debian - including the sweep that found them, which is why the count
# above came from a scan of unpacked .deb trees and not of this image. Moved
# rather than copied or symlinked: a copy is a second set of bytes to keep in
# step, and `find -type f` - which the corpus lists use deliberately, to drop
# Debian's alternatives symlinks - skips a symlink too.
RUN mv /usr/share/wine/fonts /usr/share/fonts/truetype/wine

# The four scripts an oracle driver runs, copied in rather than written with
# printf inside a RUN. The first draft did use printf, and its \x27 escapes
# reached the file as literal backslash-x-2-7 - a quoting layer between the
# check and the fact it checks, which is the same reason `unicode`'s ICU image
# compiles a version program instead of parsing icuinfo's XML.
COPY fonttools-version fonttools-corpus fonttools-corpus-bitmap \
     fonttools-corpus-ebdt fonttools-corpus-variable fonttools-corpus-cff2 \
     /usr/local/bin/
RUN chmod +x /usr/local/bin/fonttools-version \
      /usr/local/bin/fonttools-corpus \
      /usr/local/bin/fonttools-corpus-bitmap \
      /usr/local/bin/fonttools-corpus-ebdt \
      /usr/local/bin/fonttools-corpus-variable \
      /usr/local/bin/fonttools-corpus-cff2
